course: dictionary
lesson: 755
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «The boat sailed into the bay, where the water was calm.» Boat and water tell you that bay is part of the sea.
explain: «The shopper parked in the bay near the door.» Shopper and parked point to a marked space, not water. Ask yourself: what meaning fits here? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: «The wolves began to bay at the moon.» Wolves are animals, so bay here is a verb: to howl with a long, deep sound. Same spelling, different meaning, because the context is different.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Bay» can mean part of the sea or a lake that curves into the land.
fact: «Bay» can also mean a marked space for a special use, such as a parking bay or loading bay.
fact: «To bay» means to howl with a long, deep sound, like a dog or wolf.
fact: A bay tree has shiny leaves, and bay leaves are used in cooking.
exercise: «Where hard and soft rock lie side by side, the soft rock wears back into bays.» What does bay mean in this sentence? | A marked parking space | A long, deep howl | A reddish-brown horse | *A curve of the coast where the land bends inwards
exercise: «Cook landed at Botany Bay in 1770.» Which clue tells you Botany Bay is near the sea? | Cook | *Landed | Century | Claimed
exercise: «The dog began to bay at the moon.» What does bay mean here? | A parking space | A reddish-brown horse | A part of the sea | *To howl with a long, deep sound
test: «Mum put two bay leaves in the soup.» What does bay mean here? | A reddish-brown horse | *A shiny leaf from a bay tree, used in cooking | A marked space for parking | A howl like a wolf
test: «The sea was calm inside the bay, away from the big waves.» Where is the bay? | A tree in a garden | To howl at the moon | A loading space for lorries | *Part of the sea that curves into the land
exam: «In Shark Bay, Australia, dolphins wear sea sponges on their snouts.» Which meaning fits Shark Bay? | *A part of the sea that curves into the land | A reddish-brown horse | A parked car space | To howl like a dog
